window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.1","title":"Sampling Variability and Sampling Distributions","goal":"Draw 1,000 samples from a skewed population and watch the sample means form a bell-shaped sampling distribution centred on the true mean.","question":"A survey team wants to know how far one sample mean can stray from the truth. We build a made-up population of 100,000 right-skewed values (true mean about 30, true SD about 15) so that we KNOW the truth, then draw 1,000 samples of 30 and ask: how do the sample means behave?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Build a population","text":"A made-up population of 100,000 values, skewed to the right (a few large values), with a true mean near 30 and a true SD near 15, like the lesson's builder. The seed makes the numbers repeatable.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\npopulation = 15 + rng.exponential(15, size=100_000)\nmu = population.mean()\nsigma = population.std()\n\nprint(f'population mean (mu)   = {mu:.2f}')\nprint(f'population SD (sigma)  = {sigma:.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for fast arithmetic on lists of numbers, under the short name np.","Load matplotlib's plotting tools as plt; we need them later to draw the histogram.","Create a random number generator with seed 42, so everyone who runs this gets the same numbers.","Build 100,000 right-skewed values: 15 plus an exponential draw. Skew means many small values and a few big ones.","Compute the population mean mu. In real life we never know it; here we do, so we can check our work.","Compute the population SD sigma (spread of individual values), the number the standard error will be built from.","Print mu with 2 decimals; the f-string puts the number inside the text and :.2f rounds it.","Print sigma the same way, so both true values sit next to each other."],"meaning":"The made-up population has a true mean of 30.05 and a true SD of 15.08. These are the 'answers' we pretend not to know. Every sample we draw next will try to estimate 30.05, and we can see how well it does.","out":"population mean (mu)   = 30.05\npopulation SD (sigma)  = 15.08","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw 1,000 samples of size 30","text":"Each row below is one random sample of 30 people. We compute the mean of every row, so we end up with 1,000 different sample means. That pile of means is the sampling distribution.","code":"n = 30\nsamples = rng.choice(population, size=(1000, n))\nsample_means = samples.mean(axis=1)\n\nprint('first five sample means:', sample_means[:5].round(2))\nprint('number of sample means:', len(sample_means))","explain":["Set the sample size: every pretend survey will ask 30 people.","rng.choice picks values at random from the population; size=(1000, n) makes 1,000 rows, each one a sample of 30.","Take the mean of each row (axis=1 means 'across the columns'), giving 1,000 sample means.","Show the first five sample means so we can see that every sample gives a slightly different answer.","Confirm we really have 1,000 sample means; that pile of numbers is the sampling distribution."],"meaning":"The first five sample means are 30.09, 34.36, 30.63, 27.59 and 31.43. Each is an honest estimate of the true 30.05 but none is exactly right. That wobble from sample to sample is sampling variability.","out":"first five sample means: [30.09 34.36 30.63 27.59 31.43]\nnumber of sample means: 1000","figs":[]},{"title":"Centre and spread","text":"The sample means should centre on mu, and their spread (the standard error) should be close to sigma divided by the square root of n.","code":"print(f'mean of the sample means = {sample_means.mean():.2f}  (mu = {mu:.2f})')\nprint(f'SD of the sample means    = {sample_means.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'theory sigma / sqrt(n)    = {sigma / np.sqrt(n):.2f}')","explain":["Average the 1,000 sample means and compare with mu; if samples are unbiased they should centre on the truth.","Take the SD of the sample means. ddof=1 is the sample version of SD; this number is the standard error.","Work out the theory value sigma divided by the square root of n, to see if the simulation agrees with the formula."],"meaning":"The sample means average 29.99, very close to the true 30.05, so the sample mean is not systematically too high or low. Their SD is 2.83, close to the formula's 2.75. A single mean of 30 people is typically within about 3 of the truth.","out":"mean of the sample means = 29.99  (mu = 30.05)\nSD of the sample means    = 2.83\ntheory sigma / sqrt(n)    = 2.75","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the sampling distribution","text":"A histogram shows the shape. Even though the population is skewed, the sample means pile up in a fairly symmetric bell around mu. The dashed line marks mu.","code":"plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.hist(sample_means, bins=30, color='steelblue', edgecolor='white')\nplt.axvline(mu, color='black', linestyle='--', label='population mean')\nplt.xlabel('sample mean (n = 30)')\nplt.ylabel('number of samples')\nplt.title('Sampling distribution of the mean')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","explain":["Start a new figure, 6 by 3.4 inches, a good size for a lesson page.","Draw a histogram of the 1,000 sample means in 30 bars, so we can see the shape of the sampling distribution.","Add a dashed vertical line at mu, to see whether the pile is centred on the truth.","Label the horizontal axis so a reader knows each value is a mean of 30 readings.","Label the vertical axis: the height of a bar counts how many samples gave a mean in that range.","Give the picture a title that names what it shows.","Show the legend, which explains what the dashed line is.","Display the finished figure."],"meaning":"The bars form a fairly symmetric bell centred on the dashed line at 30.05, even though the population itself is skewed. Most sample means land between about 25 and 35. Averaging has tamed the skew, which is the idea behind the Central Limit Theorem.","out":"","figs":["4.1-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"set.seed(42)\npopulation <- 15 + rexp(100000, rate = 1/15)\nmeans <- replicate(1000, mean(sample(population, 30, replace = TRUE)))\nmean(population)\nmean(means)\nsd(means)\n15 / sqrt(30)\nhist(means, breaks = 30)","expect":"mean(population) about 30.05; mean(means) about 30; sd(means) about 2.8; 15 / sqrt(30) = 2.738 (Python: 30.05, 29.99, 2.83, 2.75)","note":"Random draws differ between R and Python, so simulated numbers are close but not identical; the theory value 15 / sqrt(30) = 2.74 is exact.","explain":["Fix the random seed so the simulation can be repeated exactly in R.","Build 100,000 skewed values: 15 plus exponential draws with mean 15 (rate = 1/15).","replicate repeats 'draw 30 values and take their mean' 1,000 times; sample(..., replace = TRUE) picks from the population.","Print the population mean, the value the sample means should centre on.","Print the mean of the 1,000 sample means; it should be close to the population mean.","Print the SD of the sample means, which is the standard error.","Print the theory value 15 / sqrt(30), the standard error the formula predicts.","Draw a histogram of the sample means with 30 bars to see the bell shape."],"read":"Look at the five numbers R prints. mean(means) should be close to mean(population) (both near 30), and sd(means) close to 15 / sqrt(30) = 2.74. The histogram should look like a bell. Values differ slightly from Python because the random numbers differ."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (with Random Number Functions), then Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives","syntax":"SET SEED=42.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP s = 1 TO 1000.\nCOMPUTE total = 0.\nLOOP i = 1 TO 30.\nCOMPUTE total = total + 15 + RV.EXP(1/15).\nEND LOOP.\nCOMPUTE xbar = total/30.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=xbar /STATISTICS=MEAN STDDEV.","note":"This draws each sample straight from the skewed model instead of a stored population, so the mean of xbar is near 30 and its SD near 2.7, close to but not exactly the Python numbers.","explain":["Fix the random seed so the run can be repeated.","INPUT PROGRAM lets us build a data set from nothing; there is no file to open.","Outer loop: one pass for each of the 1,000 samples.","Start this sample's running total at zero.","Inner loop: 30 draws, one for each person in the sample.","Add one skewed value (15 plus an exponential draw) to the total.","Close the inner loop after the 30 draws.","Divide the total by 30 to get this sample's mean, xbar.","END CASE writes one row (one sample mean) into the new data set.","Close the outer loop.","Tell SPSS the new data set is complete.","End the input program; from here on SPSS treats xbar as an ordinary column.","Ask for the mean and SD of the 1,000 sample means."],"read":"In the Descriptive Statistics table, the Mean of xbar should be near 30 and the Std. Deviation near 2.7 (the standard error). That matches Python up to random noise: SPSS makes its own random numbers."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset seed 42\nset obs 1000\ngenerate xbar = 0\nforvalues i = 1/30 {\n    quietly replace xbar = xbar + (15 + rexponential(15)) / 30\n}\nsummarize xbar\ndisplay 15 / sqrt(30)\nhistogram xbar","note":"Each of the 1,000 rows gets the mean of 30 fresh draws; the Mean and Std. dev. of xbar should be near 30 and 2.7, close to but not identical to Python.","explain":["Clear any data in memory so we start fresh.","Fix the random seed so the run can be repeated.","Create 1,000 empty rows, one for each sample.","Create the variable xbar and start it at 0; we will add to it.","Repeat the next step 30 times, once for each person in a sample.","In each row add one fresh skewed draw divided by 30, so after 30 passes xbar is that row's sample mean.","Close the loop.","Print the mean and SD of xbar, which are the centre and standard error of the sampling distribution.","Print the theory standard error 15 / sqrt(30) for comparison.","Draw a histogram of xbar to see the bell shape."],"read":"In the summarize table, Mean should be near 30 and Std. dev. near 2.7. The display line shows 2.738, the formula value. The histogram should look bell-shaped. Numbers are close to, not identical to, the Python output."},"conclusion":"Single readings from this population swing widely (SD about 15), but means of 30 readings cluster tightly around the true mean: 29.99 against 30.05, with a spread of about 2.8, as sigma divided by the square root of n predicts. Caution: this used a simulated population where we knew the truth; with real data we have just one sample and must trust this pattern.","practice":{"scenario":"A call centre records how long (minutes) each of 50,000 customer calls lasts. Most calls are short and a few are very long. A supervisor will time a random sample of 25 calls and wants to know how reliable the average of such a sample would be.","data":"No list to paste: build the population with rng = np.random.default_rng(11); times = 2 + rng.exponential(6, size=50_000). Then draw 2,000 samples of 25 calls.","tasks":["Find the true mean and SD of the 50,000 call times.","Draw 2,000 samples of 25 calls and find the mean and SD of the 2,000 sample means.","Compare that SD with sigma / sqrt(25).","Compare the skewness of single calls with that of the sample means."],"hint":"Copy the shape of block 2 and 3 of the lab: rng.choice with size=(2000, 25), then mean(axis=1). scipy's stats.skew measures skewness.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nrng = np.random.default_rng(11)\ntimes = 2 + rng.exponential(6, size=50_000)\nmu, sigma = times.mean(), times.std()\nmeans = rng.choice(times, size=(2000, 25)).mean(axis=1)\nprint(f'mu = {mu:.2f}, sigma = {sigma:.2f}')\nprint(f'mean of 2000 sample means = {means.mean():.2f}')\nprint(f'SD of sample means = {means.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'theory sigma/sqrt(25) = {sigma / 5:.2f}')\nprint(f'skewness: single = {stats.skew(times):.2f}, means = {stats.skew(means):.2f}')","answer":"The true mean is 7.98 minutes with an SD of 5.99. The 2,000 sample means average 7.98 and have an SD of 1.19, matching sigma / sqrt(25) = 1.20. So an average of 25 calls is usually within about 1.2 minutes of the truth. Skewness drops from 2.01 for single calls to 0.40 for the means, so the means are much closer to a bell shape.","out":"mu = 7.98, sigma = 5.99\nmean of 2000 sample means = 7.98\nSD of sample means = 1.19\ntheory sigma/sqrt(25) = 1.20\nskewness: single = 2.01, means = 0.40","figs":[]}}});
